That’s a nice way to put it: a defined undefined shape.

What I take you to mean is that the exact geometry isn’t fixed, but the rules that generate it are.

For example:

* A snowflake is never the same twice, yet we recognize “snowflake-ness.”
* A tree has no canonical outline, yet we instantly recognize one.
* A coastline has no single defining curve, but it has characteristic statistical structure.
* A Bananagrams board has no predetermined shape, yet it grows by a consistent set of constraints.

In mathematics and computer science, this idea shows up in several places:

* Generative systems: the rules are fixed, the instances vary.
* Attractors: many different starting points converge toward similar forms.
* Fractals: local rules produce endlessly varied global structures.
* Graphs: the topology matters more than the embedding in space.

So the “shape” isn’t the outline—it’s the invariant pattern of relationships.

You could even express it as:

Identity = Constraints + Degrees of Freedom

The constraints define what the system is. The degrees of freedom determine the many ways it can appear.

That’s a recurring theme in nature. DNA doesn’t specify the exact position of every leaf on a tree. It encodes developmental rules that generate a family of possible trees. Likewise, a language doesn’t prescribe every sentence—it defines the grammar from which infinitely many sentences can emerge.

So a “defined undefined shape” is one whose identity lives in the generator, not the instance. The pattern persists even though every realization is unique.